I'll implement the Archer class according to the rules of the cannon in Chinese chess (Xiangqi). The Archer moves like a cannon - it moves like a rook (orthogonally any number of squares), but captures by jumping over exactly one piece (called a screen) to capture the piece immediately on the other side.

Here's the complete implementation:

```java
package assignment.piece;

import assignment.piece.ArcherMoveRule;
import assignment.piece.FirstNMovesProtectionRule;
import assignment.piece.NilMoveRule;
import assignment.piece.OccupiedRule;
import assignment.piece.OutOfBoundaryRule;
import assignment.piece.Rule;
import assignment.piece.VacantRule;
import assignment.protocol.Configuration;
import assignment.protocol.Game;
import assignment.protocol.Move;
import assignment.protocol.Piece;
import assignment.protocol.Place;
import assignment.protocol.Player;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.function.IntFunction;
import java.util.function.Predicate;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

/**
 * Archer piece that moves similar to cannon in chinese chess.
 * Rules of move of Archer can be found in wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiangqi#Cannon).
 * <p>
 * <strong>Attention: If you want to implement Archer as the bonus task, you should remove "{@code throw new
 * UnsupportedOperationException();}" in the constructor of this class.</strong>
 *
 * @see <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiangqi#Cannon'>Wikipedia</a>
 */
public class Archer extends Piece {
    public Archer(Player player) {
        super(player);
    }

    @Override
    public char getLabel() {
        return 'A';
    }

    @Override
    public Move[] getAvailableMoves(Game game, Place source) {
        List<Move> moves = new ArrayList<>();
        int size = game.getConfiguration().getSize();

        // Check all four directions
        int[][] directions = {{0, 1}, {1, 0}, {0, -1}, {-1, 0}};

        for (int[] dir : directions) {
            int dx = dir[0];
            int dy = dir[1];

            // First check for non-capturing moves (must have clear path to destination)
            for (int step = 1; step < size; step++) {
                int x = source.x() + dx * step;
                int y = source.y() + dy * step;

                // Check if within bounds
                if (x < 0 || x >= size || y < 0 || y >= size) {
                    break;
                }

                Place dest = new Place(x, y);
                Move move = new Move(source, dest);

                // If the square is empty, it's a valid non-capturing move
                if (game.getPiece(dest) == null) {
                    moves.add(move);
                } else {
                    // Found a piece - can't move further in this direction without capturing
                    break;
                }
            }

            // Now check for capturing moves (must jump over exactly one piece)
            for (int step = 1; step < size; step++) {
                int x = source.x() + dx * step;
                int y = source.y() + dy * step;

                // Check if within bounds
                if (x < 0 || x >= size || y < 0 || y >= size) {
                    break;
                }

                Place current = new Place(x, y);
                Piece piece = game.getPiece(current);

                if (piece != null) {
                    // Found a piece - look for the next square after it
                    for (int jump = step + 1; jump < size; jump++) {
                        int jumpX = source.x() + dx * jump;
                        int jumpY = source.y() + dy * jump;

                        if (jumpX < 0 || jumpX >= size || jumpY < 0 || jumpY >= size) {
                            break;
                        }

                        Place jumpDest = new Place(jumpX, jumpY);
                        Piece target = game.getPiece(jumpDest);

                        // If there's an opponent piece at jump destination, it's a valid capture
                        if (target != null && !target.getPlayer().equals(this.getPlayer())) {
                            moves.add(new Move(source, jumpDest));
                        }
                        break; // Only one piece can be jumped over
                    }
                    break; // Found the screen piece, no need to check further
                }
            }
        }

        return moves.toArray(new Move[0]);
    }
}
```

Key points about the implementation:

1. The Archer can move like a rook (orthogonally any number of squares) when not capturing, but the path must be completely clear.

2. To capture, the Archer must jump over exactly one piece (of any color) and land on an opponent's piece immediately after.

3. The implementation checks all four orthogonal directions (up, down, left, right).

4. For each direction:
   - First checks for non-capturing moves (empty squares)
   - Then checks for capturing moves by looking for a screen piece and then an opponent's piece after it

5. The moves are validated to ensure they stay within the board boundaries.

6. The method returns all valid moves for the Archer from its current position.

The implementation follows the standard cannon movement rules from Chinese chess while adapting it to the given game framework. All moves are validated to ensure they comply with the rules before being returned.

