I'll complete the Archer class implementation based on the rules of cannon movement in Chinese chess (Xiangqi). The Archer moves like a cannon - it moves orthogonally any number of squares like a rook, but captures by jumping over exactly one piece (called a screen) in between.

Here's the complete implementation:

```java
package assignment.piece;

import assignment.protocol.Game;
import assignment.protocol.Move;
import assignment.protocol.Piece;
import assignment.protocol.Place;
import assignment.protocol.Player;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Archer piece that moves similar to cannon in chinese chess.
 * Rules of move of Archer can be found in wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xiangqi#Cannon).
 */
public class Archer extends Piece {
    public Archer(Player player) {
        super(player);
    }

    @Override
    public char getLabel() {
        return 'A';
    }

    @Override
    public Move[] getAvailableMoves(Game game, Place source) {
        List<Move> validMoves = new ArrayList<>();
        int size = game.getConfiguration().getSize();

        // Check all four directions
        checkDirection(game, source, 1, 0, size, validMoves);  // Right
        checkDirection(game, source, -1, 0, size, validMoves); // Left
        checkDirection(game, source, 0, 1, size, validMoves);  // Down
        checkDirection(game, source, 0, -1, size, validMoves); // Up

        return validMoves.toArray(new Move[0]);
    }

    private void checkDirection(Game game, Place source, int dx, int dy, int size, List<Move> validMoves) {
        int x = source.x() + dx;
        int y = source.y() + dy;
        boolean foundScreen = false;

        while (x >= 0 && x < size && y >= 0 && y < size) {
            Place destination = new Place(x, y);
            Piece pieceAtDestination = game.getPiece(destination);

            if (!foundScreen) {
                // Before finding a screen, can move to empty squares
                if (pieceAtDestination == null) {
                    validMoves.add(new Move(source, destination));
                } else {
                    foundScreen = true;
                }
            } else {
                // After finding a screen, can only capture the first piece behind it
                if (pieceAtDestination != null) {
                    if (pieceAtDestination.getPlayer() != this.getPlayer()) {
                        validMoves.add(new Move(source, destination));
                    }
                    break; // Can't jump over multiple pieces
                }
            }

            x += dx;
            y += dy;
        }
    }
}
```

Key points about the implementation:

1. The Archer moves orthogonally (up, down, left, right) like a rook.
2. For movement (non-capturing):
   - It can move to any empty square along its path until it hits another piece
   - It cannot jump over pieces when moving

3. For capturing:
   - Must jump over exactly one piece (screen) of any color
   - Can then capture the first piece after the screen
   - Cannot jump over multiple pieces
   - Cannot capture pieces of the same color

4. The implementation checks each direction separately:
   - First checks for empty squares (valid moves)
   - When it encounters a piece, it marks that as a screen
   - Then looks for the first piece after the screen (valid capture)

5. The method handles edge cases:
   - Board boundaries
   - Same-color pieces (cannot capture)
   - Multiple pieces in a row (cannot jump over)

This implementation follows the cannon movement rules from Xiangqi while adapting it to the given game framework. The Archer can move freely to empty squares or capture by jumping over exactly one intervening piece.

