import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Knight piece that moves similar to knight in chess.
 * Rules of move of Knight can be found in wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knight_(chess)).
 *
 * @see <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knight_(chess)'>Wikipedia</a>
 */
public class Knight extends Piece {
    public Knight(Player player) {
        super(player);
    }

    @Override
    public char getLabel() {
        return 'K';
    }

    /**
     * Returns an array of moves that are valid given the current place of the piece.
     * Given the {@link Game} object and the {@link Place} that current knight piece locates, this method should
     * return ALL VALID {@link Move}s according to the current {@link Place} of this knight piece.
     * All the returned {@link Move} should have source equal to the source parameter.
     * <p>
     * Hint: you should consider corner cases when the {@link Move} is not valid on the gameboard.
     * Several tests are provided and your implementation should pass them.
     * <p>
     * <strong>Attention: Student should make sure all {@link Move}s returned are valid.</strong>
     *
     * @param game   the game object
     * @param source the current place of the piece
     * @return an array of available moves
     */
    @Override
    public Move[] getAvailableMoves(Game game, Place source) {
        List<Move> validMoves = new ArrayList<>();
        int x = source.x();
        int y = source.y();

        // All possible knight moves (L-shaped)
        int[][] moves = {
            {x+1, y+2}, {x+2, y+1},
            {x+2, y-1}, {x+1, y-2},
            {x-1, y-2}, {x-2, y-1},
            {x-2, y+1}, {x-1, y+2}
        };

        for (int[] move : moves) {
            int newX = move[0];
            int newY = move[1];

            // Check if move is within board boundaries
            if (newX >= 0 && newX < game.getConfiguration().getSize() && 
                newY >= 0 && newY < game.getConfiguration().getSize()) {

                Place destination = new Place(newX, newY);
                Move potentialMove = new Move(source, destination);

                // Check if destination is vacant or contains opponent's piece
                Piece destPiece = game.getPiece(destination);
                if (destPiece == null || !destPiece.getPlayer().equals(this.getPlayer())) {
                    validMoves.add(potentialMove);
                }
            }
        }

        return validMoves.toArray(new Move[0]);
    }
}
```

The implementation:

1. Creates all 8 possible L-shaped moves a knight can make in chess
2. For each potential move:
   - Checks if the destination is within the board boundaries
   - Creates a Move object from source to destination
   - Checks if the destination is either empty or contains an opponent's piece
   - If valid, adds the move to the list of valid moves
3. Returns the valid moves as an array

The implementation follows the standard chess knight movement rules while respecting the game boundaries and piece ownership rules. It ensures that:
- The knight cannot move outside the board
- The knight cannot capture its own pieces
- All valid L-shaped moves are considered

The method returns only moves that are valid according to the basic movement rules of a knight in chess. Additional game-specific rules (like protection rules) would be handled by the game engine when actually executing the moves.

