package assignment.piece;

import assignment.protocol.Game;
import assignment.protocol.Move;
import assignment.protocol.Place;
import assignment.protocol.Player;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Knight piece that moves similar to knight in chess.
 * Rules of move of Knight can be found in wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knight_(chess)).
 *
 * @see <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knight_(chess)'>Wikipedia</a>
 */
public class Knight extends Piece {
    public Knight(Player player) {
        super(player);
    }

    @Override
    public char getLabel() {
        return 'K';
    }

    /**
     * Returns an array of moves that are valid given the current place of the piece.
     * Given the {@link Game} object and the {@link Place} that current knight piece locates, this method should
     * return ALL VALID {@link Move}s according to the current {@link Place} of this knight piece.
     * All the returned {@link Move} should have source equal to the source parameter.
     * <p>
     * Hint: you should consider corner cases when the {@link Move} is not valid on the gameboard.
     * Several tests are provided and your implementation should pass them.
     * <p>
     * <strong>Attention: Student should make sure all {@link Move}s returned are valid.</strong>
     *
     * @param game   the game object
     * @param source the current place of the piece
     * @return an array of available moves
     */
    @Override
    public Move[] getAvailableMoves(Game game, Place source) {
        List<Move> validMoves = new ArrayList<>();
        int boardSize = game.getConfiguration().getSize();
        int x = source.x();
        int y = source.y();

        // All possible knight moves (L-shaped)
        int[][] possibleMoves = {
            {x + 2, y + 1}, {x + 1, y + 2},
            {x - 1, y + 2}, {x - 2, y + 1},
            {x - 2, y - 1}, {x - 1, y - 2},
            {x + 1, y - 2}, {x + 2, y - 1}
        };

        for (int[] move : possibleMoves) {
            int newX = move[0];
            int newY = move[1];

            // Check if the move is within board boundaries
            if (newX >= 0 && newX < boardSize && newY >= 0 && newY < boardSize) {
                Place destination = new Place(newX, newY);
                Move potentialMove = new Move(source, destination);

                // Check if the destination is vacant or contains opponent's piece
                Piece destinationPiece = game.getPiece(destination);
                if (destinationPiece == null || !destinationPiece.getPlayer().equals(this.getPlayer())) {
                    validMoves.add(potentialMove);
                }
            }
        }

        return validMoves.toArray(new Move[0]);
    }
}
```

The implementation:

1. Creates a list to store valid moves
2. Gets the board size from game configuration
3. Defines all 8 possible L-shaped knight moves
4. For each possible move:
   - Checks if it's within board boundaries
   - Creates a Move object if valid
   - Checks if destination is vacant or contains opponent's piece
   - Adds valid moves to the list
5. Returns the valid moves as an array

The implementation follows standard chess knight movement rules while respecting board boundaries and piece ownership. It ensures that:
- All moves stay within the board
- The knight can't capture its own pieces
- The knight can move to empty squares or capture opponent's pieces
- All returned moves have the correct source position

The method handles all edge cases including moves that would go off the board and moves that would land on friendly pieces.

