```java
package assignment.piece;

import assignment.protocol.Game;
import assignment.protocol.Move;
import assignment.protocol.Piece;
import assignment.protocol.Place;

/**
 * The rule of moving of Archer, which is similar to the moving rule of cannon in Chinese chess.
 * Cannons move like chariots, any distance orthogonally without jumping, but can only capture by jumping a single piece of either colour along the path of attack.
 */
public class ArcherMoveRule implements Rule {
    @Override
    public boolean validate(Game game, Move move) {
        if (!(game.getPiece(move.getSource()) instanceof Archer)) {
            return true;
        }

        Place source = move.getSource();
        Place destination = move.getDestination();
        Piece sourcePiece = game.getPiece(source);
        Piece destinationPiece = game.getPiece(destination);

        // Check if move is orthogonal (same row or same column)
        if (source.x() != destination.x() && source.y() != destination.y()) {
            return false;
        }

        // Calculate direction and distance
        int dx = Integer.compare(destination.x(), source.x());
        int dy = Integer.compare(destination.y(), source.y());
        int distance = Math.max(Math.abs(destination.x() - source.x()), 
                               Math.abs(destination.y() - source.y()));

        int piecesCount = 0;

        // Count pieces between source and destination
        for (int i = 1; i < distance; i++) {
            int x = source.x() + dx * i;
            int y = source.y() + dy * i;
            Piece piece = game.getPiece(x, y);
            if (piece != null) {
                piecesCount++;
            }
        }

        // If destination is empty, there should be no pieces in between
        if (destinationPiece == null) {
            return piecesCount == 0;
        }
        // If destination has a piece (capture), there must be exactly one piece in between
        else {
            // Can only capture opponent's pieces
            if (destinationPiece.getPlayer() == sourcePiece.getPlayer()) {
                return false;
            }
            return piecesCount == 1;
        }
    }

    @Override
    public String getDescription() {
        return "archer move rule is violated";
    }
}
```

This implementation:

1. First checks if the piece being moved is actually an Archer (if not, returns true since this rule doesn't apply)
2. Verifies the move is orthogonal (same row or same column)
3. Calculates the direction and distance of the move
4. Counts the number of pieces between the source and destination:
   - If moving to an empty square, there must be no pieces in between (like a rook)
   - If capturing, there must be exactly one piece in between (like a cannon)
5. For captures, also verifies the destination piece belongs to the opponent

The rule follows the cannon movement pattern from Chinese chess where:
- When moving without capturing, it moves like a rook (no jumping)
- When capturing, it must jump over exactly one piece (of any color) to capture the opponent's piece

