package assignment.piece;

import assignment.protocol.Game;
import assignment.protocol.Move;
import assignment.protocol.Place;

/**
 * Moving rule of Knight in chess (no block)
 * It moves two squares vertically and one square horizontally, or two squares horizontally and one square vertically
 */
public class KnightMoveRule implements Rule {

    @Override
    public boolean validate(Game game, Move move) {
        if (!(game.getPiece(move.getSource()) instanceof Knight)) {
            return true;
        }

        Place source = move.getSource();
        Place destination = move.getDestination();

        int xDiff = Math.abs(destination.x() - source.x());
        int yDiff = Math.abs(destination.y() - source.y());

        // Knight moves in an L-shape: (2,1) or (1,2)
        return (xDiff == 2 && yDiff == 1) || (xDiff == 1 && yDiff == 2);
    }

    @Override
    public String getDescription() {
        return "knight move rule is violated";
    }
}
```

This implementation:

1. First checks if the piece being moved is actually a Knight (if not, returns true since this rule doesn't apply)
2. Gets the source and destination places from the move
3. Calculates the absolute differences in x and y coordinates between source and destination
4. Validates that the move follows the knight's L-shaped movement pattern:
   - Either 2 squares in one direction and 1 square in the perpendicular direction (xDiff=2 and yDiff=1)
   - Or 1 square in one direction and 2 squares in the perpendicular direction (xDiff=1 and yDiff=2)
5. Returns true if the move is valid, false otherwise
6. Provides a description message for when the rule is violated

The knight is unique in chess as it's the only piece that can "jump" over other pieces, so we don't need to check for blocking pieces in this rule.

